{"path":"atomic/atomic/operator=","title":"std::atomic<T>::operator=","titleHtml":"<span class=\"t-title-prefix\">std::atomic&lt;T&gt;::</span>operator=","originalUrl":"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/atomic/atomic/operator=","section":"atomic","kind":"page","summary":"Unlike most assignment operators, the assignment operators for atomic types do not return a reference to their left-hand arguments.","html":"<table class=\"t-dcl-begin\"><tbody>\n<tr class=\"t-dcl t-since-cxx11\" id=\"Version_1\">\n<td> <div><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">T operator<span class=\"sy1\">=</span><span class=\"br0\">(</span> T desired <span class=\"br0\">)</span> <span class=\"kw1\">noexcept</span><span class=\"sy4\">;</span></span></div></td>\n<td> (1) </td>\n<td> <span class=\"t-mark-rev t-since-cxx11\">(since C++11)</span> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"t-dcl t-since-cxx11\" id=\"Version_2\">\n<td> <div><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">T operator<span class=\"sy1\">=</span><span class=\"br0\">(</span> T desired <span class=\"br0\">)</span> <span class=\"kw4\">volatile</span> <span class=\"kw1\">noexcept</span><span class=\"sy4\">;</span></span></div></td>\n<td> (2) </td>\n<td> <span class=\"t-mark-rev t-since-cxx11\">(since C++11)</span> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"t-dcl t-since-cxx11\" id=\"Version_3\">\n<td> <div><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">atomic<span class=\"sy3\">&amp;</span> operator<span class=\"sy1\">=</span><span class=\"br0\">(</span> <span class=\"kw4\">const</span> atomic<span class=\"sy3\">&amp;</span> <span class=\"br0\">)</span> <span class=\"sy1\">=</span> delete<span class=\"sy4\">;</span></span></div></td>\n<td> (3) </td>\n<td> <span class=\"t-mark-rev t-since-cxx11\">(since C++11)</span> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"t-dcl t-since-cxx11\" id=\"Version_4\">\n<td> <div><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">atomic<span class=\"sy3\">&amp;</span> operator<span class=\"sy1\">=</span><span class=\"br0\">(</span> <span class=\"kw4\">const</span> atomic<span class=\"sy3\">&amp;</span> <span class=\"br0\">)</span> <span class=\"kw4\">volatile</span> <span class=\"sy1\">=</span> delete<span class=\"sy4\">;</span></span></div></td>\n<td> (4) </td>\n<td> <span class=\"t-mark-rev t-since-cxx11\">(since C++11)</span> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"t-dcl-sep\"><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<div class=\"t-li1\"><span class=\"t-li\">1,2)</span> Atomically assigns <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">desired</span></code> to the atomic variable. Equivalent to <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">store<span class=\"br0\">(</span>desired<span class=\"br0\">)</span></span></code>.\n<table class=\"t-rev-begin\">\n<tr class=\"t-rev t-since-cxx20\"><td>\n<p>It is deprecated if <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\"><a href=\"/w/cpp/atomic/atomic\"><span class=\"kw2083\">std::<span class=\"me2\">atomic</span></span></a><span class=\"sy1\">&lt;</span>T<span class=\"sy1\">&gt;</span><span class=\"sy4\">::</span><span class=\"me2\">is_always_lock_free</span></span></code> is <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\"><span class=\"kw2\">false</span></span></code> and overload <span class=\"t-v\">(2)</span> participates in overload resolution.\n</p>\n</td>\n<td><span class=\"t-mark-rev t-since-cxx20\">(since C++20)</span></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"t-li1\"><span class=\"t-li\">3,4)</span> Atomic variables are not <a href=\"/w/cpp/named_req/CopyAssignable\"><em class=\"t-named-req\">CopyAssignable</em></a>.</div>\n<h3 id=\"Parameters\">Parameters</h3>\n<table class=\"t-par-begin\">\n<tr class=\"t-par\">\n<td> desired\n</td>\n<td> -\n</td>\n<td> value to assign\n</td></tr></table>\n<h3 id=\"Return_value\">Return value</h3>\n<div class=\"t-li1\"><span class=\"t-li\">1,2)</span> <code class=\"t-c\"><span class=\"mw-geshi cpp source-cpp\">desired</span></code></div>\n<h3 id=\"Notes\">Notes</h3>\n<p>Unlike most assignment operators, the assignment operators for atomic types do not return a reference to their left-hand arguments. They return a copy of the stored value instead.\n</p>\n<h3 id=\"See_also\">See also</h3>\n<table class=\"t-dsc-begin\">\n<tr class=\"t-dsc\">\n<td>  <div class=\"t-dsc-member-div t-dsc-member-nobold-div\"><div><a href=\"/w/cpp/atomic/atomic/atomic\"> <span class=\"t-lines\"><span>(constructor)</span></span></a></div></div>\n</td>\n<td>  constructs an atomic object <br> <span class=\"t-mark\">(public member function)</span> </td></tr>\n</table>","headings":[{"depth":3,"text":"Parameters","id":"Parameters"},{"depth":3,"text":"Return value","id":"Return_value"},{"depth":3,"text":"Notes","id":"Notes"},{"depth":3,"text":"See also","id":"See_also"}],"breadcrumbs":[{"path":"","title":"C++ reference"},{"path":"atomic","title":"Concurrency support library (since C++11)"},{"path":"atomic/atomic","title":"std::atomic"}],"children":[],"map":{"label":"std::atomic<T>::operator=","children":[{"label":"Declarations","note":"4 declarations; revisions: since C++11. Atomically assigns desired to the atomic variable. Equivalent to store(desired)."},{"label":"Parameters","note":"desired"},{"label":"Return value","note":"desired"},{"label":"Notes","note":"Unlike most assignment operators, the assignment operators for atomic types do not return a reference to their left-hand arguments."},{"label":"See also","children":[{"label":"(constructor)","note":"constructs an atomic object"}]}]},"standards":["C++11","C++20"],"wordCount":122,"codeBlockCount":0}
